		(property "Description" "Resistor"
			(at 138.43 60.96 0)
			(hide yes)
			(show_name no)
			(do_not_autoplace no)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
			)
		)
		(property "Note" "IR RECEIVER SUPPLY FILTER, series element. RETAINED at 100 R after audit in FBV2-S1-007. The topology matches the Vishay application circuit exactly - R1 in series with VS and C1 from VS to ground - and the datasheet recommends it wherever there is ripple or spikes on the supply. Vishay prints the topology but no values, so the values are ours and are justified here. With C11 4.7 uF the corner is 1 / (2 pi x 100 x 4.7u) = 339 Hz, which is 41 dB of attenuation at the 38 kHz carrier. THAT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS: datasheet Fig. 7 shows the receiver threshold irradiance starts degrading at roughly 10 mV RMS of supply ripple AT THE CARRIER FREQUENCY and has doubled by about 50 mV, and our own transmitter runs at exactly that frequency. With 40 mV pk-pk on the rail the receiver sees about 0.1 mV RMS, roughly 90 times margin. DC drop is 0.45 mA x 100 R = 45 mV, so VS sits at about 3.25 V, comfortably inside 2.0 to 5.5 V."
			(at 138.43 60.96 0)
			(hide yes)
			(show_name no)
			(do_not_autoplace no)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
			)
		)
		(pin "2"
			(uuid "2127dbae-d105-43d6-a37a-e95d08d901b6")
		)
